          "text": "{\"translation_en\":null,\"larger_context_summary\":\"Meg March speaks to her mother, Marmee, at the close of Meg’s wedding to John Brooke, then turns to her father and the rest of the family. Marriage marks her departure from the March household and raises the emotional prospect that her new bond with John might weaken her place within her family. Meg reassures them that she will remain closely involved, promising frequent visits and imagining her sisters sharing in her new domestic life. Her tearfulness and clinging reflect the bittersweet stakes of leaving home while preserving familial intimacy.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The occurrence is primarily an assertion about the continuing strength of Meg’s affection, which makes its strongly truth-conditional classification persuasive. Its syntax is especially valuable: Meg does not simply declare “I love you,” but asks Marmee not to believe that she loves her “any the less” because she loves John so much. The negated, embedded form therefore preserves two simultaneous relationships rather than presenting romantic love as replacing filial love. Although Meg clings to her mother with tear-filled eyes, that emotional intensity belongs to the delivery and situation; it does not make the clause a spontaneous or reflexive expressive outburst. Nor does the love clause itself create an obligation or alter the relationship performatively. The nearby assurance that Meg will visit every day has a more commitment-like character, but that force should not be transferred to this avowal. The wording reports and reassures about an enduring emotional state.\"}"
